      "matchText": "Indo-Islamic",
      "term": "Indo-Islamic rule",
      "definition": "Indo-Islamic rule refers to the roughly five and a half centuries (from about 1206) in which Muslim dynasties — the Delhi Sultanate's Khalji and Tughlaq lines, the Deccan's Bahmani kingdom, and ultimately the Mughals — governed large parts of the Indian subcontinent, fusing Persian-Islamic statecraft, art, and language with Indian society."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Land revenue",
      "term": "Land revenue",
      "definition": "Land revenue was the tax on agricultural production — the assessed value of the land's output, collected in cash — that formed the single largest source of the British colonial state's income in India through the late 19th century, levied under regionally varying systems such as Bengal's Permanent Settlement and the south's ryotwari."
    },
